-----------------------------------------------------------------------
HOW EACH TEST WORKS
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

  Every deck runs the sampler TWICE on the same polygon:

    1. with no_connect   -> raw_<deck>.inp   the sampler's own points
    2. with connect      -> out_<deck>.inp   the finished mesh

  Spacing is measured on the RAW points. The finished mesh cannot be
  used for that: after connecting, the driver smooths it (moving
  interior points) and recon adds points on the boundary, so it no
  longer shows what the sampler produced.

  Element quality is measured on the finished mesh.

  The connect/no_connect token is REQUIRED on the createpts line.
  Leaving it off is an input error.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------
RESULTS
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

  PASS      no crash, no ERROR, no WARNING (the polygons are convex),
            both output files written, and the HARD checks pass:
              - every sampled point inside or on the polygon
              - no inverted triangles
              - no triangle naming a point that does not exist
  REFUSED   a deck marked "expect : error" was refused, as intended
  FAIL      anything else

  The exit status is nonzero on any FAIL.

  Everything else in the table is MEASURED, NOT JUDGED. Thresholds
  have not been calibrated yet, so these are for comparing runs and
  spotting change:

    points    number of sampled points
    density   points x h^2 / area. Near 0.7 for a well filled sample.
    sep min   smallest spacing between INTERIOR points, as a fraction
              of h. Each pair is judged against the larger of its two
              target spacings: d / max(h_p, h_q).
    close     interior points with a neighbour closer than h
    bnd cl    the same, for points on the polygon boundary. The
              sampler places boundary points closer than h by design,
              so these are counted separately rather than mixed in.
    verts     polygon corners that appear among the sampled points
    ang min   smallest angle of any triangle, degrees
    poor%     share of triangles with smallest angle below 20 deg

-----------------------------------------------------------------------
READING THE PICTURE
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

  Each test has a picture in three panels.

  LEFT     the raw sampled points (dark dots) inside the polygon.
           A RED line joins two points closer than h (pairs where
           both are on the boundary are not marked). A MAGENTA ring
           marks a point outside the polygon; there should be none.

  MIDDLE   the finished mesh, each triangle coloured by its smallest
           angle: dark red < 10, red < 20, orange < 30, light green
           < 40, green >= 40 degrees. A BLACK triangle is inverted
           (a FAIL). The BLUE dashed circle is the largest empty
           circle in the mesh: the biggest gap between points.

  RIGHT    histograms of interior spacing / h and of smallest
           triangle angle. The red dashed lines mark d = h and the
           20 degree "poor" threshold.

OUTPUT

  logs/summary.html   EVERYTHING ON ONE PAGE
  logs/<deck>.svg     one test's picture
  logs/<deck>.check   one test's full measurement report
  logs/<deck>.all     all LaGriT output for one test

-----------------------------------------------------------------------
ADDING A TEST
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

  Copy a p2d*.lgi deck and give it a new name starting with p2d. The
  runner reads three lines from its header:

      # polygon : <file>.poly
      # h       : <value>
      # expect  : valid | error

  and a deck expecting an error may add

      # reason  : <text that must appear in the ERROR message>

  A deck with no expect line FAILS, so a new test cannot go unjudged.
  The deck must dump raw_<deck>.inp and out_<deck>.inp.

  New circles and ellipses:

      python3 make_ellipse.py --out <name> --a <x semi-axis> \
              --b <y semi-axis> --h <h> --spacing 4

  --spacing sets the polygon edge length as a multiple of h. The
  existing ones use 4, so the sampler chooses most boundary points
  itself instead of having them fixed by the outline. The script
  prints the polygon's sharpest corner and largest usable h; the
  driver refuses corners under 9 degrees and h over twice the
  shortest edge.

  h is uniform in every deck so far. For a variable h field, put its
  formula in h_at() in check_poisson_2d.py.

